<header><p class="st-kicker">other · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Ireland</h1><p class="st-meta">1171 CE – 1542 CE · capital Dublin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The territory in Ireland held by English kings as lords of Ireland from Henry II's invasion of 1171 until Henry VIII took the title of king in 1542.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> in 1171 to control <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> and other Norman adventurers. Irish kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> submitted to him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> endorsed his claim. In 1177 he made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">John</a> Lord of Ireland, a title merged with the English crown when John became king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> by a justiciar or lord deputy, with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> from the 13th century, English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> and shires. Norman lords built castles and towns, but from the 14th century Gaelic recovery and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> shrank English rule to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-pale">Pale</a> around Dublin, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statutes-of-kilkenny">Statutes of Kilkenny</a> of 1366 sought to stop settlers adopting Irish ways.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1541 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> King of Ireland, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland</a> in 1542.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-ireland.jpg" alt="Lordship of Ireland" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: NsMn · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Lordship_of_Ireland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1171 CE – 1542 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dublin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry II, John, Gerald FitzGerald, 8th Earl of Kildare</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish, Anglo-Norman French, English, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>, <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dominium Hiberniae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=lordship-of-ireland" aria-label="Lordship of Ireland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4541 343 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Ireland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4861 599L4988 654M4861 599L4799 613M4861 599L4786 635M4861 599L4838 618M4861 599L4851 567M4861 599L4861 599M4861 599L4840 629M4861 599L4970 663M4861 599L4861 599M4861 599L4838 618M4861 599L4849 593M4861 599L4872 561M4861 599L4799 620M4861 599L4838 618"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4988 654h0M4799 613h0M4786 635h0M4838 618h0M4851 567h0M4861 599h0M4840 629h0M4970 663h0M4861 599h0M4838 618h0M4849 593h0M4872 561h0M4799 620h0M4838 618h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dublin. <a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=lordship-of-ireland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
